/*
 * EXAMPLE 8 — SinglyLinkedList<Integer>, singular case.
 *
 * 10 goes on the front, 20 on the back, so the two ends hold different values and
 * a library that appended both to the same end fails one of the two ensures
 * clauses.  removeFirst must then hand back 10 — the value \old(head) named — and
 * the surviving 20 must be found at position 0 by indexOf.
 *
 * removeFirst binds nothing in the inputs block: it takes no parameters, and what
 * it returns is a SERVER_OUTPUT.
 */
test LinkedListDryRun {
    sequence: addFirst -> addLast -> removeFirst -> indexOf;

    inputs {
        addFirst[0].value = 10;
        addLast[1].value = 20;
        indexOf[3].value = 20;
    }
}
